  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe area of showers currently moving across the Quad State has\nbeen much more pesky than advertised, which has resulted in a \nlot wetter day over much of the region. It has been weakening as\nit moved east, but just not as drastically as just about all\nguidance was showing, so we will expect it to continue to\nweaken through the remainder of the area, but most places will\nat least see some light rain. The positive from all of this, is\nthat destabilization will struggle behind it over southeast\nMissouri. \n\nAs for the severe threat tonight, the CAMs are generally\nadvertising a pretty significant severe storm environment over\nMissouri, but the eastern extent of it is in doubt after today's\nsystem. With MLCAPE 1000-2500J/kg, 0-3km SRH 200-400m2/s2, and\n0-1km SRH 150-200m2/s2, supercells with damaging winds and a\ntornado threat are a strong possibility. \n\nLooking at obs over Missouri, a boundary has set up from central\nMO south to just east of KUNO. This boundary will likely push\neastward into our far western areas through the remainder of the\nday, meanwhile the cold front which is already into west central\nMissouri will continue southeast. Each of these boundaries are\nlikely focal points for thunderstorm development. For our area,\nI would expect storms to move quickly south along the\nboundary/eastern edge of the instability. The timing is still \nnot clear, but should still be this evening, and most likely \nthe latter half of the evening. The showers today have been very\nefficient rain producers, so with the instability expected in \nthe far west this evening, some flash flooding cannot be ruled \nout. Hopefully, the storms will be moving fast enough to \nmitigate much of the flooding threat. \n\nFarther to the east across the area, instability really drops \noff and convection should generally be weaker. However, \nwidespread showers and storms should progress through the area \novernight along with the cold front. Some heavy rain will be \npossible, and some minor flooding issues may develop, especially\nin those poor drainage areas.\n\nMuch of Saturday will be dry behind the cold front, but\nwraparound showers and storms will spread southeast into the \nQuad State in the afternoon and continue through Sunday. No\nsevere threat is expected, but with the proximity of the upper\nlow some cold air funnels could dance around the area.\n\nHeading into next week, guidance is still not certain how far\neast the upper low will push, leaving the possibility of some\nshowers or even storms developing in the far east in the\nafternoon on Monday and Tuesday. By Wednesday it should\ndefinitely push east of the region, as high pressure aloft\nbuilds over the Quad State. The forecast dries out and high \ntemperatures should climb back to normal levels Wednesday, and \nby next Friday, highs will be in the lower 90s throughout the \nregion and heat indices will be in the 100-105 range. If \ndewpoints overachieve, as they seem to always do this summer, \nanother Heat Advisory may be needed Friday.",
